{"id":"6fead61765fa4e2eb1af65d6669aab72","model":"gpt-oss-120b","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"answer\": \"noanswer\",\n  \"citations\": []\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to answer: \"What United Kingdom Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party?\" The phrasing is ambiguous. Likely asking: Which UK Prime Minister was succeeded (i.e., replaced) in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party. In the 1990s, the Conservative Party leader became Prime Minister after succeeding a previous PM. In 1990, Margaret Thatcher was succeeded by John Major (who was Conservative Party leader). So the Prime Minister succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party: Margaret Thatcher was succeeded by John Major (who was the Conservative Party leader). However the question: \"What United Kingdom Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party?\" Could also refer to 1997 when John Major (Conservative Party leader) was succeeded by Tony Blair (Labour). But the phrase \"by the Leader of the Conservative Party\" suggests the successor is the Leader of the Conservative Party. In 1990, John Major was the Leader of the Conservative Party and succeeded Margaret Thatcher as PM. So answer: Margaret Thatcher.\n\nWe need evidence. The provided evidence includes: \"These were the first annual local elections for the new Conservative Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher.\" That's about 1980. Not about succession. There's also evidence about Federation of Conservative Students referencing Margaret Thatcher. Not about succession. There's evidence about Conservative Party leadership election 1997 triggered when John Major resigned after defeat. That shows John Major was leader and PM before 1997. But we need evidence that Margaret Thatcher was succeeded by John Major in 1990. Not present in given evidence. Let's search within evidence: There's no direct statement about Thatcher being succeeded. However we have \"The Federation of Conservative Students (FCS) was the student organisation of the British Conservative Party from the late 1940s to 1986.\" Not relevant.\n\nWe have \"The 1997 Conservative Party leadership election was triggered ... when John Major resigned ... following his party's defeat at the 1997 general election\". That indicates John Major was PM before 1997. But we need who he succeeded. Not present.\n\nWe have \"These were the first annual local elections for the new Conservative Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher.\" That indicates Thatcher was PM in 1980. Not about succession.\n\nWe have \"The Conservative Party, officially the Conservative and Unionist Party, is a centre-right political party...\" Not about succession.\n\nThus we may not have direct evidence for the answer. Could answer \"noanswer\". But maybe we can infer from evidence: The question: \"What United Kingdom Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party?\" The evidence includes that John Major was Conservative Party leader and Prime Minister until 1997. It also includes that John Major resigned in 1997 after defeat. It does not state who succeeded him. But the question asks about a Prime Minister succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party. That could be Margaret Thatcher succeeded by John Major (who was Leader of Conservative Party). Do we have evidence that John Major was Leader of Conservative Party? Not directly. The evidence about 1997 leadership election says \"when John Major resigned ... following his party's defeat\". It doesn't say he was leader. But we can infer he was leader. However we need explicit citation. Not present.\n\nThus we cannot answer with evidence. So answer \"noanswer\"."},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1716,"completion_tokens":736,"total_tokens":2452,"audio_prompt_tokens":0},"created":1791963227,"object":"chat.completion","prompt_filter_results":null}